libva-v4l2_request
==================

A VA-API (libva) backend driver for V4L2 stateless video decoders using
the Media Request API. This API and VA-API are generic APIs, and the driver
is written in such a way that it does not hardcode RK3399 reality, but
it was so far developed mostly on Pinebook Pro (RK3399) with some minor
testing done on Orange Pi 5+ (RK3588) and is thus fine-tuned for the use
on RK3399 and RK3588. It will likely need some tuning to run optimally on
other SoCs that have upstream Linux stateless video decode drivers.

Specifically on RK3588 it's impossible to support HEVC decoding using VA-API,
AFAIK, due to major, un-workaroundable API mismatch. On RK3399 the support
is very good for all codecs the HW supports. You'll be able to have HW
acceleration out of the box on Firefox and in mpv just by installing the
VA-API driver. No patching of other projects is needed. For HEVC decoding
you'll need a kernel patch for RGA2 driver, which is used to convert 10bit
NV15 pixel format to NV12 understood by unpatched upstream mpv/ffmpeg/Firefox
projects. See details below.

Supported codecs (compiled in when the kernel uAPI headers provide the
stateless interface): MPEG-2, H.264, HEVC, VP8, VP9, AV1.

On Rockchip SoCs whose decoders emit 10-bit content only as packed NV15
(which no GPU or display controller stack can import), the driver chains
the RGA into the decode pipeline and converts frames to NV12 in hardware;
see "10-bit decoding and the RGA converter" below.

Have fun,
	~megi

Building
--------

    meson setup build
    ninja -C build
    ninja -C build install     # installs into the libva driverdir

Run against a specific build without installing:

    export LIBVA_DRIVERS_PATH=/path/to/build/src
    export LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME=v4l2_request
    vainfo

Environment variables:

  LIBVA_V4L2_REQUEST_MEDIA_PATH=/dev/mediaN
      Restrict device probing to a single media controller. By default
      all /dev/media* nodes are scanned for video interfaces that
      advertise V4L2_BUF_CAP_SUPPORTS_REQUESTS.

  V4L2R_NO_CONVERT=1
      Disable detection and use of the mem2mem format converter. Streams
      that decode to a format nothing can consume (10-bit NV15) are then
      refused instead of converted.


Using with mpv
--------------

libva normally derives the driver name from the DRM driver of the render
node, which does not point at this driver on ARM SoCs (the GPU is
panfrost or similar), so the driver has to be selected explicitly:

    export LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME=v4l2_request
    mpv --hwdec=vaapi video.mp4

That is all an installed driver needs; for an uninstalled build also set
LIBVA_DRIVERS_PATH as shown in "Building" above. To select it
permanently, put LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME=v4l2_request into /etc/environment
(or set hwdec=vaapi in ~/.config/mpv/mpv.conf and export the variable
from your session startup).

Video output:

  --vo=gpu (the default): decoded frames are imported into the GPU as
      dma-bufs and composited by the render pipeline. Works with both
      Wayland and X11.
  --vo=dmabuf-wayland: fully zero-copy path, frames go to the Wayland
      compositor as dma-bufs without touching the GPU; cheapest option
      on machines like the Pinebook Pro.
  --hwdec=vaapi-copy: readback into system memory instead of dma-buf
      display; slower, but a useful fallback to isolate display interop
      problems from decode problems.

Verifying it works: mpv prints "Using hardware decoding (vaapi)" with
-v, and this driver logs its device choices to stderr, e.g.:

    libva-v4l2request: decoding h264 via /dev/video1 [rkvdec] (media /dev/media1)
    libva-v4l2request: using CAPTURE format NV12 (1920x1088)

10-bit HEVC/VP9 content decodes through the RGA conversion chain (see
below) and shows up as NV12 surfaces; an additional line reports the
converter. If mpv instead reports falling back to software decoding,
run with --msg-level=vd=debug and check stderr for driver logs and
dmesg for kernel driver errors.

Rotation offload (see "Video processing" below) is available through
the transpose_vaapi lavfi filter:

    mpv --hwdec=vaapi --vf=lavfi=[transpose_vaapi=dir=cclock] video.mp4

This is especially useful on devices with a portrait panel, like the
Pinephone Pro and its 720x1440 display: landscape video can be rotated
by the RGA and handed to the compositor as ordinary upright dma-buf
frames, keeping the zero-copy --vo=dmabuf-wayland path (with the phone
held sideways) instead of paying for a GPU rotation pass on every
frame.

Note that mpv's own --video-rotate performs rotation in the GPU shader
path instead and does not use the VA-API entrypoint.


Running mpv on the Pinephone Pro (Weston + rotation)
----------------------------------------------------

The Pinephone Pro panel is portrait 720x1440 and the VOP cannot rotate
planes, so landscape video has to be rotated somewhere along the way.
A convenient setup is a one-shot Weston kiosk session that rotates the
whole output to landscape - UI, touch input and video alike - with mpv
handing frames over as dma-bufs.

One-time setup on Arch Linux ARM: install weston and seatd, enable the
seatd service and give the user access to the devices:

    pacman -S weston seatd
    systemctl enable --now seatd
    usermod -aG video,input,seat <user>

(video covers the decoder/RGA and KMS nodes, input the touchscreen,
seat the seatd socket. Log in again for the groups to apply.)

Then, from a VT or ssh login:

    export LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME=v4l2_request

    cat > west.conf <<EOF
    [output]
    name=DSI-1
    transform=rotate-90
    EOF

    weston --config=$PWD/west.conf --shell=kiosk-shell.so -- \
        mpv --vo=dmabuf-wayland --hwdec=vaapi --fs \
            --script-opts=osc-scalefullscreen=2,osc-vidscale=no,osc-hidetimeout=2000,osc-layout=slimbox,osc-windowcontrols=no \
            video.mp4

The kiosk shell fullscreens the client and quits with it, so this acts
as a single-purpose video session. --vo=dmabuf-wayland passes decoded
NV12 frames (including 10-bit content downconverted by the RGA chain)
to Weston without copies; the osc-* options make the on-screen controls
usable on the small touchscreen.

With transform=rotate-90 the rotation happens in Weston's GL compositing
pass. To rotate on the RGA instead, drop the transform from west.conf
and add the video processing filter:

    --vf=lavfi=[transpose_vaapi=dir=clock]

The video then arrives at the compositor already upright, which keeps
the frames eligible for direct plane scanout (no GPU touch at all) -
but mpv's UI and the touch mapping stay portrait, so this suits
plain "play a file fullscreen" use rather than interactive control.

Scaling can be offloaded to the RGA the same way: scale_vaapi runs
through the same video processing entrypoint, so a 1080p stream can be
brought down to the panel size already in the filter chain:

    --vf=lavfi=[transpose_vaapi=dir=clock,scale_vaapi=720:1280]

(scale_vaapi also works on its own, without the transpose, when Weston
does the rotating.) Each filter is one RGA pass - transpose_vaapi has
no output size parameter, so rotation+scaling cannot be folded into a
single pass from ffmpeg - but the passes are cheap, and everything
downstream (GL sampling or the plane scaler) then only touches
panel-sized buffers instead of 1080p ones, which more than halves the
per-frame memory traffic on the display path - worth having on a
phone.


Using with Firefox
------------------

Firefox decodes video in its sandboxed RDD process, and the sandbox
blocks the device access this driver needs (open/ioctl on /dev/video*
and /dev/media* nodes). Launch Firefox with:

    export LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME=v4l2_request
    export MOZ_DISABLE_RDD_SANDBOX=1
    export MOZ_ENABLE_WAYLAND=1        # if not already running native Wayland

and make sure VA-API decoding is enabled in about:config:

    media.ffmpeg.vaapi.enabled = true

Note that disabling the RDD sandbox trades away some of the protection
Firefox normally applies to media decoding.


Design
------

- Each VA context owns a circular queue of 4 mmap()ed OUTPUT (bitstream)
  buffers, each permanently paired with one media request fd. Requests
  are reused after MEDIA_REQUEST_IOC_REINIT.

- Every VA surface keeps one specific CAPTURE buffer for its whole
  lifetime, so vaExportSurfaceHandle() returns a stable dma-buf per
  VASurfaceID (dma-buf consumers that cache imports by surface id, e.g.
  mpv --vo=dmabuf-wayland, depend on this). Buffers are created with
  VIDIOC_CREATE_BUFS on first use of a surface, so contexts created with
  an empty render target list (GStreamer) work.

- Frames reference each other through the V4L2 buffer timestamp, which
  encodes the CAPTURE buffer index ((index + 1) * 1000 ns, matching
  v4l2_timeval_to_ns()).

- Because the kernel matches reference frames purely by CAPTURE timestamp
  and does not stop a still-referenced buffer from being overwritten, a
  surface's buffer is not reused for a new decode until every frame that
  referenced its previous contents has been dequeued. The driver tracks,
  per CAPTURE buffer, the submission sequence of the last frame that
  referenced it and blocks the next decode into it until enough frames
  have completed. This is the same guarantee FFmpeg's hwaccel gets
  structurally from its AVBufferPool refcount.

- Multi-slice H.264/HEVC frames use V4L2_BUF_FLAG_M2M_HOLD_CAPTURE_BUF;
  H.264 queues one request per slice, HEVC batches up to the SLICE_PARAMS
  control dimension. Both honour the driver's decode mode (slice/frame
  based) and start code convention, queried from the control defaults.

- The CAPTURE queue is configured lazily on the first frame, after the
  codec controls (SPS etc.) have been set on the device, so the kernel
  driver can pick a frame format matching the stream bit depth - like
  FFmpeg's probe order.

- vaEndPicture() is asynchronous; vaSyncSurface() polls and dequeues.

- Surfaces that are exported or mapped *before* they were ever decoded
  into (mpv probes formats this way: it exports bare test surfaces with
  no decode context) get standalone dma-buf backing, allocated from a
  decoder CAPTURE queue on a throwaway fd. The backing is dropped once
  the surface is bound to a real decode context (kept when it serves as
  the format converter's destination, see below).

- CAPTURE format selection prefers linear, VAImage-representable formats
  (NV12/P010) over tiled driver defaults, so dma-buf display interop and
  readback work out of the box.

- AV1: the V4L2 interface needs refresh_frame_flags, which VA-API does
  not carry and which cannot be recovered from the tile group data. Each
  frame's submission is therefore deferred by one frame and its exact
  refresh mask reconstructed from the next frame's ref_frame_map. Reads
  of a still-held surface flush it with a heuristic mask first; pipelined
  players always get the exact value.


10-bit decoding and the RGA converter
-------------------------------------

Rockchip decoders (rkvdec and friends) output 10-bit frames only in the
packed NV15 layout (4 samples in 5 bytes). There is no VA fourcc for it,
GPU drivers cannot import it, and Wayland compositors do not accept it,
so NV15 frames are undisplayable as-is.

When a plain (non-request) V4L2 mem2mem converter that can read such a
format and write NV12 is present - the Rockchip RGA - the driver chains
it behind the decoder (the converter is probed on first need, so plain
8-bit playback never scans for one):

- 10-bit VA configs then advertise NV12 surfaces only (FFmpeg accepts
  the depth loss and decodes into NV12-labeled surfaces).
- As soon as a frame's decode completes, its CAPTURE buffer is queued to
  the converter (decoder dma-buf in, the surface's NV12 backing dma-buf
  out) and the conversion overlaps the following decodes. Readers wait
  for the conversion result; exports hand out the converted NV12 buffer.
- No pixel ever passes through the CPU: decode, 10-to-8 conversion and
  display are all zero-copy hardware paths.
- Reference frames are unaffected; the decoder keeps referencing its own
  NV15 buffers.

Without a usable converter the driver refuses to decode such streams
(the first decode fails and players fall back to software decoding);
there is no software conversion fallback.

IMPORTANT: the mainline rockchip-rga kernel driver does not support NV15
sources. The required kernel patch is included in patches/:

    patches/0001-media-rockchip-rga-Add-support-for-downconverting-fr.patch

It adds the NV15 source format, enables the hardware 10-bit unpacker and
10-to-8 rounding, and works around an RGA2 quirk where the direct (non
scaling) 10-bit fetch path mis-addresses every other 128-pixel tile.


Video processing: rotation and mirroring
----------------------------------------

The same converter backs a VAEntrypointVideoProc implementation (the
VAProfileNone profile), which performs surface-to-surface blits with
clockwise rotation by 90/180/270 degrees, horizontal/vertical mirroring,
cropping and scaling. The parameters come from the standard
VAProcPipelineParameterBuffer (rotation_state, mirror_state,
surface_region, output_region); rotation support is advertised through
vaQueryVideoProcPipelineCaps() rotation_flags/mirror_flags, as clients
expect. No parametrized filters (denoise etc.) are offered - rotation and
mirroring are pipeline parameters in VA-API, not filters.

With FFmpeg this is the transpose_vaapi filter, e.g. to rotate a phone
recording upright:

    mpv --hwdec=vaapi --vf=lavfi=[transpose_vaapi=dir=cclock] video.mp4

Notes:

- The entrypoint only exists when the converter is present; it is probed
  on first use of VAProfileNone, so decode-only clients still never scan
  for it.
- For 90/270 degree rotation the destination surface must be created with
  the transposed dimensions (FFmpeg's transpose_vaapi does this).
- When the source surface sits behind the 10-bit conversion chain, the
  blit reads the raw NV15 decoder output and folds the 10-to-8-bit
  downconversion and the rotation into a single RGA pass.
- One synchronous mem2mem job per vaEndPicture(), imported dma-buf to
  imported dma-buf; pixels never pass through the CPU.
- Both source and destination are NV12 (or NV15 internally); RGB formats
  are not wired up.


Buffer copies
-------------

Bitstream data is copied exactly once, from the VA buffer into the
mmap()ed OUTPUT buffer, at vaRenderPicture() time (start codes are
inserted per slice where the driver wants Annex-B framing, so a plain
map-through is not possible in general). The OUTPUT buffers start well
below the raw frame size and are replaced with larger ones on the rare
frame that outgrows them. Decoded frames are never copied by the CPU:

- vaExportSurfaceHandle(DRM_PRIME_2) exports the CAPTURE buffer planes
  (or the converted NV12 backing) as dma-buf fds, with DRM format
  modifiers for tiled layouts (Allwinner tiled, Broadcom SAND, ARM AFBC).
- vaDeriveImage() maps the buffer directly for linear NV12 and P010
  single-plane layouts, reporting the surface size with the real line
  stride.
- vaGetImage()/vaPutImage() are copying access paths for the same linear
  formats (full-frame, no scaling, no format conversion).


Recovered bitstream fields
--------------------------

VA-API omits several fields the V4L2 stateless interfaces require. The
driver re-parses small parts of the bitstream to recover them:

- H.264: nal_ref_idc, idr_pic_id, pic_order_cnt_bit_size,
  dec_ref_pic_marking_bit_size (RBSP slice header parse).
- HEVC: NAL unit type, temporal id, short/long_term_ref_pic_set_size,
  num_delta_pocs_of_ref_rps_idx (slice segment header parse).
- VP9: segmentation feature data, loop filter deltas, quantiser deltas
  (uncompressed header parse) and the full
  V4L2_CID_STATELESS_VP9_COMPRESSED_HDR probability updates (VPX
  boolean decoder), needed by rkvdec.
- AV1: refresh_frame_flags via one-frame deferred submission (see above);
  per-reference order hints are tracked driver-side per surface.


Known limitations
-----------------

- Decode (VAEntrypointVLD) and video processing (VAEntrypointVideoProc,
  rotation/mirroring/scaling blits only), no encode, no subpictures, no
  vaPutSurface display path.
- Streams that decode to a format with no VA representation are refused
  unless the hardware converter can handle them (see above).
- H.264 pic_order_cnt_type == 1 streams: VA-API does not transport the
  SPS offset_for_ref_frame table.
- H.264 PPS num_ref_idx_l*_default_active is taken from the first
  non-IDR P/B slice that does not override the counts (VA-API does not
  carry the real PPS defaults); only relevant for frame-based decoders
  that parse slice headers in hardware (rkvdec).
- HEVC entry point offsets are not passed (libva has no usable subsets
  buffer definition); purely a WPP performance hint for Hantro G2.
- HEVC on decoders that require the full SPS reference picture set
  tables (Rockchip rkvdec2/vdpu38x EXT_SPS_ST_RPS controls) is declined:
  VA-API never exposes the SPS st_ref_pic_set() syntax, so these devices
  cannot be driven correctly over VA-API at all.
- Tiled/compressed CAPTURE formats can be exported as dma-bufs but not
  read back with vaGetImage() (no CPU detiling).


